# Day 7 – Claude Usage Strategy

## 🚀 ABTalks 60-Day Claude AI Mastery Challenge

### 📌 Topic
Choosing the Right Claude Model & Effort Level

## 🎯 Objective

The goal of Day 7 was to understand how different Claude models and reasoning effort levels can be selected based on the type and complexity of a task.

## 🧠 What I Learned

Claude provides different models and effort levels for different types of work:

- **Haiku** – Best for fast answers, summaries, rewrites, and simple tasks.
- **Sonnet** – A balanced choice for everyday learning, coding, and general work.
- **Opus** – Useful for deep research, complex reasoning, strategy, and advanced planning.
- **Effort Levels** – Low, Standard, High, and Max can be selected depending on how much reasoning a task requires.

## 🛠️ My Claude Usage Strategy

| Task | Best Model | Effort | Reason |
|------|------------|--------|--------|
| Quick questions | Haiku | Low | Fast response |
| Learning & explanations | Sonnet | Standard | Good balance |
| Coding & debugging | Sonnet | Standard/High | Better reasoning |
| Deep research | Opus | High | Complex analysis |
| Career planning | Sonnet | High | Detailed recommendations |
| Complex strategy | Opus | High/Max | Advanced reasoning |

## 💡 Biggest Learning

The most powerful model is not always necessary.

Choosing the **right model + right effort level** for each task can improve both **output quality and efficiency**.

## ⚡ Final Recommendation

For most of my daily work, I would use **Sonnet with Standard effort** because it provides a strong balance between speed, quality, and reasoning.

I would switch to **High/Max effort or Opus** when working on complex research, strategy, or difficult problems.

